already established: @:op(+), @:const, @:prop(get, set), @:get(field), @:set(feild), @:hide

@:capture for lambda captures. default captures all as references, e.g.
    var lambda:()->Int;
    {
        var x = 0;
        lambda = function() return x; // captured by reference
    } // lambda outlives x
    lambda(); // UB


    now
    {
        var x = 0;
        lambda = @:capture(x) function() return x; // captured by value
    }
    lambda(); // return 0;

    var y = 0;
    var z = 0;
    var lambda0 =                  function() return y + z; // captures all by reference
    var lambda1 = @:capture(&y)    function() return y + z; // error: undefined identifier z
    var lambda2 = @:capture        function() return y + z; // captures nothing
    var lambda3 = @:capture(y, &z) function() return y + z; // captures y by value and z by reference
    var lambda4 = @:capture(&, z)  function() return y + z; // captures everything by reference, but z by value


    C++ generation:
    // maps 1:1
    auto lambda0 = [&]    (){ return y + z; };
    auto lambda1 = [&y]   (){ return y + z; };
    auto lambda2 = []     (){ return y + z; };
    auto lambda3 = [y, &z](){ return y + z; };
    auto lambda4 = [&, z] (){ return y + z; };

    arguments:
    - &:    everything by reference, already the default, can be overwritten
    - =:    everything by value, can be overwritten
    - &v:   capture v by reference
    - v:    capture v by value
    - none: captures nothing



@:comptime for compile time constants, mostly for NTTPs. replaced by their value at generation, e.g.
    var x = 5;
    var arr:FixedArray<Int, x>; // error: x is a runtime value

    now
    @:comptime var x = 5;
    var arr:FixedArray<Int, x>; // fine
    x = 1; // error

    C++ generation:
    int arr[5]; // replacing x

    no arguments

    note: mirrors the primary function of C++ constexpr 



@:usingTypes to specify access to real types within type parameters, e.g.
    class Array<T> {} // T is a typename
    class Array<Ptr<T>> {} // Ptr is also a typename, expecting 1 type argument, can't access outside types

    now
    @:usingTypes(Ptr)       class Array<Ptr<T>>         {} // becomes a pattern, Ptr refers to its type. This overload is used when there is a array of pointers
    @:usingTypes            class Array<Buffer<Ptr<T>>> {} // instead of @:usingTypes(Ptr, Buffer), has access to all types, T is undefined elsewhere so it is seen as a typename
    @:usingTypes(Undefined) class Array<Undefined>      {} // error

    C++ generation: generates 1:1 using type template overloading

    arguments:
    - none:  includes all types
    - types: only names

    note: NTTPs are not affected as they have access to all types already and cannot define new parameters



@:deduced for generic params, e.g.
    class FixedArray<T, SIZE:Int> {}
    class Other<T, SIZE:Int, arr:FixedArray<T, SIZE:Int>> {}
    
    // has to be used like
    @:comptime var arr = [0, 1, 2];
    var other:Other<Int, 3, arr>;

    now
    // with deduced
    class Other<@:deduced T, @:deduced SIZE:Int, arr:FixedArray<T, SIZE:Int>> {} // tells the resolver to not store it in the param list
    var other:Other<arr>; // T and SIZE are inferred

    class Array<@:deduced T> {}
    var arr:Array<Int>; // error

    C++ generation: generates 1:1 using type template overloading

    no arguments



@:requires for generic param requirenments
    class Buffer<T, SIZE:Int> {} // for small buffers

    var x:Buffer<Int, 8>;
    var x:Buffer<Int, 128>; // same buffer, same treatment

    now
    class Buffer<T, SIZE:Int> @:requires(SIZE > 0 && SIZE <= 16)  {} // for smaller buffers
    class Buffer<T, SIZE:Int> @:requires(SIZE > 16)               {} // for bigger buffers

    var x:Buffer<Int, 8>;  // small buffer
    var x:Buffer<Int, 64>; // big buffer

    C++ generation: generates 1:1 to simple uses of the C++ requires keyword

    arguments: strictly 1 expression, preferably logic gates



@:primal to mark built-in types and disallow duplicated declarations and overloads for a specific type
    // in std
    class Int {}
    class Float {}

    // in user created module
    class Int {} // allowed
    class Float {}

    now
    // in std
    @:primal class Int {}
    @:primal class Float {}

    // in user created module
    class Int {} // error: duplicated definition of primal type
    @:primal class Float {} // same error from std module and this one

    C++ generation: none, compile time only

    no arguments

    note: can also be used to disallow duplicated definition of a user defined type across the whole project




@:destructor, mimics C++ ~T method
    class SmartPtr<T> { /* ... */ }

    {
        var ptr:SmartPtr<Int> = Mem.malloc(64);
    } // memory leak

    now
    class SmartPtr<T> {
        // ...
        @:destructor inline function destroy() free(this);
    }

    {
        var ptr:SmartPtr<Int> = Mem.malloc(64);
    } // calls destroy

    C++ generation: generates 1:1 using C++ destructors

    no arguments




@:copy for copying an expression before using it
    var vec = Vec3(0, 0, 0);
    var other = vec;

    other.x = 330; // also modifies vec.x

    now
    var vec = Vec3(0, 0, 0);
    var other = @:copy vec;

    other.x = 330; // does not modify vec

    // we can also do
    var other = vec.copy();
    var other = Vec3(vec);

    C++ generation: calls the type's copy constructor

    no arguments 



@:bitfield for flag enums
    enum Flags {
        FLAG0 = 0x0   // each one has to be explicitly assigned
        FLAG1 = 0x1
        FLAG2 = 0x2
        FLAG3 = 0x4
        FLAG4 = 0x8
        FLAG5 = 0x10
        FLAG6 = 0x20
        FLAG7 = 0x40
    }

    now
    @:bitfield enum Flags { // starts counting in power of 2
        FLAG0; // 0x0
        FLAG1; // 0x1
        FLAG2; // 0x2
        FLAG3; // 0x4
        FLAG4; // 0x8
        FLAG5; // 0x10
        FLAG6 = 0x80
        FLAG7; // 0x100 continues from last
    }

    C++ generation: generates an enum with those values

    no arguments